 ## Overview
-Fine-tuning in 3D Gaussian Splatting refers to a per-scene optimization process that refines an initial set of 3D Gaussian primitives to reconstruct a specific scene with high fidelity [S1, S3]. It addresses the problem of initializing a scene representation from sparse or noisy data (such as a generated point cloud or a feed-forward prediction) by treating the reconstruction as a continuous optimization problem supervised by rendered image losses [S1, S3]. Unlike static initialization methods, fine-tuning actively adjusts geometry, color, and opacity attributes, often enabling dynamic densification (growing and pruning) to adapt the primitive count to the scene's complexity [S3]. It sets itself apart from global training strategies by focusing on optimizing a single, fixed scene representation from a pre-initialized state, rather than learning a generalizable mapping from inputs to outputs [S1, S3].
